1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,844
Tuesday shows negative twenty dollars in the daily report.

2
00:00:03,844 --> 00:00:05,369
Is that a load failure?

3
00:00:05,369 --> 00:00:12,792
We will account for every cent using seven payment events and build a revenue view that explains all three days.

4
00:00:12,792 --> 00:00:14,077
A row is one event.

5
00:00:14,077 --> 00:00:18,606
Order five hundred and one has both a capture and a partial refund.

6
00:00:18,606 --> 00:00:22,933
Keeping only one row per order would lose a real money movement.

7
00:00:22,933 --> 00:00:26,854
A decline is an observation for audit, but no money moved.

8
00:00:26,854 --> 00:00:33,133
Open the repository in V S Code, sync dependencies, and change to the first lesson.

9
00:00:33,133 --> 00:00:36,008
Compose starts a frontend and backend.

10
00:00:36,008 --> 00:00:39,639
Setup creates the database and seeds seven rows.

11
00:00:39,639 --> 00:00:45,540
In DataGrip, use the My S Q L driver, localhost and port nine zero three zero.

12
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:46,953
Compute two amounts.

13
00:00:46,953 --> 00:00:49,566
Captures contribute to gross revenue.

14
00:00:49,566 --> 00:00:53,733
Refunds are recorded as positive cents in their own column.

15
00:00:53,733 --> 00:00:56,276
Subtract once to obtain net revenue.

16
00:00:56,276 --> 00:01:02,421
Now predict what happens to Wednesday if we remove all declined events before grouping.

17
00:01:02,421 --> 00:01:06,631
Monday has two hundred dollars in captures and twenty in refunds.

18
00:01:06,631 --> 00:01:12,266
Tuesday has sixty dollars in captures and an eighty dollar refund for an earlier order.

19
00:01:12,266 --> 00:01:16,670
Wednesday has just one decline, so the row exists with zero revenue.

20
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:20,491
A day with no events would need a calendar table to appear.

21
00:01:20,491 --> 00:01:22,702
Build the view and run the check.

22
00:01:22,702 --> 00:01:27,189
Try filtering declines out before the aggregation and run it again.

23
00:01:27,189 --> 00:01:28,797
Which date went missing?

24
00:01:28,797 --> 00:01:31,476
Restore the formula and check once more.

25
00:01:31,476 --> 00:01:34,222
The worked solution is in the repository.

26
00:01:34,222 --> 00:01:39,112
Being able to explain the negative day and the zero day is the real test.
